1
00:00:00,950 --> 00:00:06,750
Running your own OpenChronology server
takes one application and one PostgreSQL

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:12,900
database, with an extension that stores
times as exact rational numbers.

3
00:00:12,950 --> 00:00:16,750
Put it behind your usual
HTTPS proxy, and browsers,

4
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,526
phones and the desktop
app all connect to it.

5
00:00:22,256 --> 00:00:25,316
Every release comes with
server packages for Debian,

6
00:00:25,366 --> 00:00:30,236
Ubuntu, Rocky Linux and
RHEL, Fedora, FreeBSD,

7
00:00:30,286 --> 00:00:35,066
macOS through Homebrew, and Windows,
plus a Docker Compose setup for anything

8
00:00:35,116 --> 00:00:38,354
else, each with checksums to verify.

9
00:00:40,084 --> 00:00:45,794
Check the download, then install the
package. On first install it creates its

10
00:00:45,844 --> 00:00:50,714
service user, its configuration and a
database with the extension in place,

11
00:00:50,764 --> 00:00:55,234
and starts the server. With
Docker, it's one compose command,

12
00:00:55,284 --> 00:00:58,442
and the database runs
in its own container.

13
00:01:00,202 --> 00:01:06,332
Then make it yours, in one configuration
file. Set the address it's served at,

14
00:01:06,382 --> 00:01:12,732
and a name for your site. The first
administrator account is created from it.

15
00:01:12,782 --> 00:01:16,742
Register Google, GitHub or Facebook
if you want those sign-ins,

16
00:01:16,792 --> 00:01:22,129
and add email, which turns on password
recovery and two-factor authentication.

17
00:01:23,759 --> 00:01:27,259
Day to day, the administration
pages cover accounts,

18
00:01:27,309 --> 00:01:33,369
storage limits, featured timelines, and
a queue of what readers have reported.

19
00:01:33,419 --> 00:01:38,069
One thing to tell your users: private
timelines are hidden from other users,

20
00:01:38,119 --> 00:01:41,202
but not from you, the
people who run the server.

21
00:01:42,682 --> 00:01:47,532
Upgrading is the same three steps
every time: back up the database,

22
00:01:47,582 --> 00:01:50,892
install the new package,
and run the migration,

23
00:01:50,942 --> 00:01:54,452
which updates the schema
and restarts the server.

24
00:01:54,502 --> 00:01:58,410
Backups are yours to schedule,
as with any database.

25
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:03,870
The packages and the guides
are with every release.

26
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:06,960
Install it for your school,
your team or your community,

27
00:02:07,010 --> 00:02:10,048
and the timelines stay on your own server.
